+# Secure Federated Design & Threat Model (Adobe Aegis Starter)
+
+Goals:
+- Protect client training updates (confidentiality), ensure integrity of aggregated results, and resist poisoning attacks.
+
+Threats:
+- Passive eavesdropper on network (mitigation: mTLS)
+- Curious or compromised server (mitigation: threshold secure aggregation; do not reveal per-client updates)
+- Malicious clients sending poisoned updates (mitigation: robust aggregation, contribution clipping, anomaly detection)
+- Replay or sybil attacks (mitigation: enrollment tokens, attestation, rate limits)
+
+Recommendations:
+- Use vetted secure aggregation libraries (TFF secure aggregation, OpenMined / PySyft)
+- Enforce client enrollment via Vault-issued JWTs and optional device attestation
+- Use contribution clipping and outlier detection prior to aggregation
+- Apply DP after aggregation using a vetted library (diffprivlib or PyDP) and document epsilon/delta
+- Log round metadata and participant lists to Rekor for non-repudiable audit
+
+Testing:
+- Simulate Byzantine clients and large-scale noise injection
+- Run privacy & utility evaluation to select epsilon budget
+- Third-party security review and pen-testing before production launch
